WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.403
Hello and welcome to mCoding.

00:00:01.403 --> 00:00:02.400
I'm James&nbsp;Murphy.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.982
Let's get going talking about descriptors.

00:00:04.982 --> 00:00:06.540
Contrary to how the name might&nbsp;sound,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.780
descriptors are not the 
 mutual enemy of Autobots and Decepticons,

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.872
nor do they have anything to do with descriptions.

00:00:12.872 --> 00:00:14.340
Descriptors in Python are&nbsp;

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.580
somewhat of a feature&nbsp;that's&nbsp;
 hidden in plain sight.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.220
Officially, an object is a descriptor if it has&nbsp;

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.760
any of&nbsp;these dunder 
 `get`,&nbsp;`set`, or `delete` methods.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.223
And their purpose is to allow you to&nbsp;

00:00:25.223 --> 00:00:28.680
customize&nbsp;what it means to get,&nbsp;
 set, or delete an attribute.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.700
In this case, `x` is an instance 
 of the&nbsp;descriptor on the left.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.180
When you call `obj.x`, this 
 calls the descriptor's `get`&nbsp;method.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.140
When you call `obj.x = something`,
 that calls the `set` method.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.520
And when&nbsp;you call `del obj.x`, that
 calls the `delete` method.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.460
`get` takes the object and&nbsp;the object type.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.620
Importantly, this allows&nbsp;you to do different things

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.760
based on whether something was called&nbsp;
 from an instance or the class itself.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.340
If this is your first time
hearing&nbsp;about descriptors in Python,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.520
you're probably thinking this 
 is one of those&nbsp;very niche features.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:00.840
It's advanced use only.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.775
And, by the way, isn't that the same thing&nbsp;
 as `getattr`, `setattr`, and `delattr`?

00:01:05.775 --> 00:01:08.520
Why is there a&nbsp;need for descriptors at all?

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.315
It is true that without knowing 
 more&nbsp;about the internals of your class,

00:01:12.315 --> 00:01:15.180
`something.x` could be calling the&nbsp;`getattr`,

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.770
or it could be calling the&nbsp;
`get` method of a descriptor.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.760
`obj.x&nbsp;= something` could&nbsp;
 be calling the `setattr`,

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.920
or it could be calling the `set` of a descriptor.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.840
And `del obj.x` could be calling
the `delattr`, or a `delete` of a descriptor.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.913
Without seeing&nbsp;the internals
of the class, you just can't tell.

00:01:30.913 --> 00:01:33.540
But there's a big difference&nbsp;
between `getattr`, `setattr`,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.214
and `delattr` versus the descriptor versions.

00:01:36.214 --> 00:01:39.973
Namely, the methods on the right-hand&nbsp;
side are defined per class.

00:01:39.973 --> 00:01:44.080
Whereas, the ones on the
left-hand side&nbsp;are defined per attribute.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.242
On the right, it's the class that's
determining how to&nbsp;access attributes.

00:01:48.242 --> 00:01:52.868
Whereas, on the left, it's the attribute
itself that&nbsp;determines how it's accessed.

00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:54.848
Even still, you may be thinking:

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:57.180
"Descriptors? I'm never&nbsp;going to need that, right?"

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.240
"I never define descriptors, I&nbsp;never work&nbsp;with descriptors."

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.716
"I don't need to know."

00:02:01.716 --> 00:02:04.800
Well, welcome to my list
of descriptors hiding in&nbsp;plain sight.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.119
You may not have realized it,
but you're using them all the time.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:11.040
In descriptor number one, functions.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.720
Have you ever&nbsp;noticed a difference that you get

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.080
when you access a function
through an instance versus through&nbsp;the class?

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.000
Accessing the function `f` through an
instance little , `a`, we get a bound method.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.905
But&nbsp;accessing the same `f` through
the class itself gives us a function object.

00:02:25.905 --> 00:02:29.220
It's the same `f` in&nbsp;both
cases, but I'm getting different results.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.280
That's because every function you define
with the&nbsp;`def` keyword is a descriptor

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.871
that defines a `get` method.

00:02:33.871 --> 00:02:36.600
And it uses the descriptor&nbsp;
protocol to do something different

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.034
based on whether it was called
from an instance or the class&nbsp;itself.

00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:41.760
Functions in Python are written in C.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.260
So this isn't exactly what it's doing.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.000
But&nbsp;it's something close to this.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.819
Every function has a `get` method.

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:50.160
If the object is `None`, then&nbsp;there's
no instance associated with this lookup,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.857
meaning it was called from a class object itself.

00:02:52.857 --> 00:02:55.560
In that case, we just return the function as is.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.200
Otherwise, `object` isn't `None`,&nbsp;
and we're in a case like this,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.497
where we're looking up a function
on an actual instance&nbsp;variable.

00:03:01.497 --> 00:03:03.720
In that case, instead of&nbsp;
returning the function itself,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.260
we return some kind of&nbsp;bound
function that remembers the object.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.425
So, yeah, if you're using functions,&nbsp;
then you're using descriptors.

00:03:10.425 --> 00:03:13.680
Number two, descriptor hiding&nbsp;
in plain sight,&nbsp;properties.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.053
And yes, this is a different case
than just functions. Let me show you why.

00:03:17.053 --> 00:03:21.449
Let's reach&nbsp;into the class's dictionary
and print out what this `area` thing actually is.

00:03:21.449 --> 00:03:23.040
I'm accessing it&nbsp;through the dictionary&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.067
like this in order to avoid&nbsp;
invoking the descriptor.

00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:28.140
We see that `area` is&nbsp;not actually a function.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.780
It's a property object.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.777
First, `area` is defined as&nbsp;
a function just like&nbsp;normal.

00:03:33.777 --> 00:03:37.140
Then, you replace `area` with
whatever you get by calling `property` on it.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.988
That's&nbsp;why technically, `area`
is a property object, not a function.

00:03:40.988 --> 00:03:45.660
So, this is exactly the same&nbsp;as this.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.100
And property is a descriptor.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.529
So, it controls what it does&nbsp;
when you say `dot area`.

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:53.283
It just so happens that what it
does is call that original `area` function.

00:03:53.283 --> 00:03:55.140
Properties like this&nbsp;are never really needed.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.193
You could always just call the function directly.

00:03:57.193 --> 00:03:59.160
But it's a common way&nbsp;to indicate to the&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.500
programmer that this thing&nbsp;
is really cheap to compute.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.780
By making `area` a&nbsp;property, you're basically&nbsp;

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.240
telling users that `area`&nbsp;
is so cheap to compute that

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.771
it's basically&nbsp;as free as an attribute access.

00:04:08.771 --> 00:04:11.460
If for some reason your
`area` function really was expensive,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.080
then&nbsp;make it look expensive.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.416
Don't hide the fact that
it's a function call. Just make that explicit.

00:04:16.416 --> 00:04:19.740
Funnily enough, the main reason
that I see people use properties is actually

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:23.942
to introduce a feature&nbsp;to Python
that was specifically left out of the language.

00:04:23.942 --> 00:04:26.932
By design, in Python, all attributes&nbsp;are public.

00:04:26.932 --> 00:04:28.860
There's no way to prevent someone from&nbsp;

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.993
accessing internal implementation&nbsp;
details of&nbsp;your classes.

00:04:31.993 --> 00:04:34.920
And aside from inheriting
from a built-in type like `tuple`,

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.980
this also makes it impossible&nbsp;
to make truly immutable types.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.780
But this is a pretty common pattern to&nbsp;

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.571
prevent people&nbsp;from accidentally&nbsp;
mutating your object.

00:04:42.571 --> 00:04:45.120
Add an underscore to the
beginning of your attribute&nbsp;name.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.240
Then make a property with
the same name without the underscore.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.700
People can still read the&nbsp;name, no problem.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.680
But if they try to write to it, they get an error.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.600
But of course, this isn't true&nbsp;immutability.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.382
Someone could just reach inside and
manually change the underscore variable name.

00:04:58.382 --> 00:05:00.540
But&nbsp;it's pretty much an unspoken rule in Python

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.720
that if you have an underscore
variable or underscore&nbsp;function,

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.240
then you're not meant to touch those.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.480
So if you do change an underscore
variable or&nbsp;call an underscore function,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.379
then you should expect
everything to break. It's your own&nbsp;fault.

00:05:12.379 --> 00:05:15.546
Anyway, here's how you might
implement property if you were doing it yourself.

00:05:15.546 --> 00:05:18.707
The&nbsp;built-in property also does
`set` and `delete`, but you get the idea.

00:05:18.707 --> 00:05:22.563
As per usual, if you&nbsp;weren't passed
an instance, then just return the property itself.

00:05:22.563 --> 00:05:27.240
Otherwise, call the stored function&nbsp;
on the instance that was passed in.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:31.000
Hidden descriptor number three,
class methods&nbsp;and static methods.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.780
Both class and static methods
allow you to call a function

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:36.480
whether you have&nbsp;an
instance of the class or the class itself.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.180
In both cases, since you
might not have an instance&nbsp;to work with,

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:40.740
there's no `self` parameter.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.980
And the difference is that a static
method has no&nbsp;implied parameters.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.055
Whereas, a class method
has an implied class parameter.

00:05:47.055 --> 00:05:48.420
So in both of these&nbsp;cases,

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.780
whether you called with the capital
animal class or the lowercase animal instance,

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:57.169
the class&nbsp;parameter of the `create`
function will be filled in with the animal class.

00:05:57.169 --> 00:05:59.647
I have a whole video on class&nbsp;
methods versus static methods.

00:05:59.647 --> 00:06:01.268
Check that out if you want to hear more.

00:06:01.268 --> 00:06:04.889
As far as possible&nbsp;implementations go,
they could look something like this.

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:08.919
Just like properties, both of these take and&nbsp;
remember the function that they're applied to.

00:06:08.919 --> 00:06:10.320
Static method is much simpler.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.178
Whether you were&nbsp;called with an instance
or not, just always return the function back.

00:06:14.178 --> 00:06:18.300
Class method is a bit trickier because&nbsp;
we need to supply that class parameter.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.260
If we weren't passed the type to use,
then we just&nbsp;use the type of the object.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.354
Then this is how we bind
that object to the class parameter.

00:06:25.354 --> 00:06:28.017
Remember,&nbsp;functions are descriptors.

00:06:28.017 --> 00:06:30.720
And the `get` method returns
a bound version of the function

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.063
where&nbsp;the first argument
is bound to the first argument of the `get`.

00:06:35.063 --> 00:06:38.280
It's not totally clear if the&nbsp;
second argument matters at all.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.540
But this works.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:45.530
So as you can see, descriptors are often
used&nbsp;to make sort of function object-like things.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.604
But that's not all they're useful&nbsp;for.

00:06:47.604 --> 00:06:50.549
Let's take a look at number four, slots.

00:06:50.549 --> 00:06:54.360
This is another one that I have a full&nbsp;
video on, but here's the quick rundown.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.480
Normally, objects have an instance dictionary.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.134
Anytime you store a variable into the&nbsp;

00:06:59.134 --> 00:07:02.108
object, it really just stores&nbsp;
it inside this dictionary.

00:07:02.108 --> 00:07:04.320
But especially for small objects, dictionaries&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.987
aren't necessarily the most&nbsp;
efficient way to&nbsp;store things.

00:07:06.987 --> 00:07:08.880
If you define `__slots__ = ['x', 'y', 'z']`,

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.160
then you're saying the only three&nbsp;attributes&nbsp;

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.113
that my instances are going&nbsp;
to have are `x`, `y`, and `z`.

00:07:14.113 --> 00:07:17.222
You can get, set, and delete&nbsp;
`x`, `y`, and `z` no problem.

00:07:17.222 --> 00:07:20.760
But if you try to get, set,
or delete `W`, then you get an&nbsp;error.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.740
Once again, directly reaching inside the&nbsp;

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.254
classes dictionary, we see&nbsp;
that `X` is a member&nbsp;object.

00:07:26.254 --> 00:07:29.789
These `__slots__` members also
define all three of `get`, `set`, and `delete`.

00:07:29.789 --> 00:07:32.583
And because&nbsp;there's no
instance dictionary to manage this,

00:07:32.583 --> 00:07:36.420
these `get`, `set`, and `delete` have
to reach&nbsp;into the underlying C structure

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:39.405
of the objects and manually modify them.

00:07:39.405 --> 00:07:42.234
Again, see my&nbsp;video
on slots if you'd like to hear more.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.960
And speaking of instance dictionaries,&nbsp;
do you know what else are descriptors?

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.760
Instance dictionaries.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:53.145
The dunder `__dict__` attribute of&nbsp;any class
that has instance dictionaries isn't a dictionary.

00:07:53.145 --> 00:07:54.889
It's an attribute object, which is&nbsp;a&nbsp;

00:07:54.889 --> 00:07:57.874
descriptor with all three of&nbsp;
`get`, `set`, and `delete`.

00:07:57.874 --> 00:08:02.478
And notice this weird idiom that I had to&nbsp;
do in order to see this attribute object.

00:08:02.478 --> 00:08:06.957
I had to reach into the dictionary
of the class&nbsp;and then read the dictionary.

00:08:06.957 --> 00:08:10.320
If you print out just the `__dict__`,
it looks like a dictionary.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:15.122
But if you look at the type, you see
that it's actually a mapping proxy object.

00:08:15.122 --> 00:08:18.435
This happens&nbsp;because,
remember, `__dict__` is a descriptor.

00:08:18.435 --> 00:08:22.688
So accessing `E.__dict__` invokes the descriptor.

00:08:22.688 --> 00:08:27.188
And what the descriptor does is return
this proxy object instead of the actual attribute.

00:08:27.188 --> 00:08:29.916
So that's&nbsp;why we had to
do this double `__dict__` thing.

00:08:29.916 --> 00:08:34.120
Moving on, how about a
real-world library use&nbsp;case of descriptors?

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:39.303
Look no further than one of the most
popular Python packages of all time,&nbsp;SQLAlchemy.

00:08:39.303 --> 00:08:44.580
SQLAlchemy lets you communicate
with databases through Python in a pythonic way.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:49.260
Here,&nbsp;I define a user account
table with two fields, ID and name.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.840
When I define the class, I say&nbsp;that ID is a column&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:55.448
that takes integers, and name&nbsp;
is a column that takes strings.

00:08:55.448 --> 00:08:59.520
When&nbsp;I operate on an actual instance
of the class, I'm working not with column objects.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:01.878
But with&nbsp;actual ints and strings.

00:09:01.878 --> 00:09:04.680
Getting different behavior
on a class versus an instance?

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.897
You guessed it, they're descriptors.

00:09:06.897 --> 00:09:11.280
Number seven. Another common
use for descriptors is field&nbsp;validation.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:15.608
For example, I want to say here, this
item has a price that's greater than zero.

00:09:15.608 --> 00:09:17.340
Whenever I set a price, I want to make&nbsp;

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:20.160
sure that it's positive,&nbsp;
and if not, I want an error.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.560
This functionality is
accomplished in the `set` method.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.260
Before setting the attribute,&nbsp;
check if it's bigger than zero.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:26.700
Otherwise, throw a `ValueError`.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.711
Then proceed with setting&nbsp;the attribute.

00:09:28.711 --> 00:09:32.706
This is an interesting use
case for Python's `__set_name__`.

00:09:32.706 --> 00:09:34.800
This function&nbsp;is called at&nbsp;
class construction time.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:38.604
And its purpose is to let
each object know what&nbsp;its name is.

00:09:38.604 --> 00:09:40.320
After the class body runs, this&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.720
`greater_than` object will be&nbsp;
told that its&nbsp;name is `price`

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.760
by having the `__set_name__` called with the&nbsp;

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.692
owner being the `Item` class&nbsp;and&nbsp;
the name being `price`.

00:09:47.692 --> 00:09:49.860
In this case, I'll prepend&nbsp;
an underscore to the name.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:54.360
And then use that as the sort of private
location where I'm storing the actual data.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:57.280
So the descriptor is stored at `item.price`.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:03.193
But the value that's underlying the price, the&nbsp;
actual price, gets stored at `item._price`.

00:10:03.193 --> 00:10:06.420
Knowing the name that we're assigned&nbsp;
to is a good way to avoid conflicts

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:09.498
if you have multiple of these
descriptors in the same class.

00:10:09.498 --> 00:10:11.640
So if I also had a `quantity` field, then there&nbsp;

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.800
wouldn't be any fight over&nbsp;
where to store each of&nbsp;them.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.890
They each have their own private location.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:19.002
And the final descriptor on&nbsp;
my list is super&nbsp;lookups.

00:10:19.002 --> 00:10:22.560
Suppose I have a `Package` class
that can ship to some address.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.343
And an `ExpressPackage`&nbsp;that ships faster.

00:10:25.343 --> 00:10:27.660
I don't really recommend doing this.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:32.160
But you can set a base view onto your&nbsp;class.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.656
Create a super object, and stick it on there.

00:10:34.656 --> 00:10:38.084
If you ship an `ExpressPackage`,&nbsp;
it's on the way right away.

00:10:38.084 --> 00:10:42.186
But if you call `ship` on the base
view, then you'll get the&nbsp;parent's behavior.

00:10:42.186 --> 00:10:43.800
I'm not going to go into why this works here.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:46.920
I have a whole video on `super` if&nbsp;
you want to see the gory details.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.140
This next portion is slightly more&nbsp;advanced&nbsp;

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.574
and deals with some&nbsp;
tricky&nbsp;issues you might run into.

00:10:51.574 --> 00:10:55.200
So if&nbsp;a descriptor's `get` method
and a class's `getattr` method

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:59.400
can both define&nbsp;what it means&nbsp;
to say `object.something`,

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.565
then what happens if you&nbsp;
have a class that&nbsp;has both?

00:11:02.565 --> 00:11:04.260
I encourage you to take this example.

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:08.880
And try commenting and uncommenting&nbsp;things
to see how things actually work.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.340
The first trip-up is that&nbsp;
there's not just&nbsp;one `getattr`.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:13.725
There's `getattr`, and `getattribute`.

00:11:13.725 --> 00:11:18.480
And `getattribute` is actually the one that's&nbsp;
more similar to `setattr` and `delattr`.

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.157
`getattribute`, `setattr`, and&nbsp;
`delattr` are&nbsp;always called.

00:11:23.157 --> 00:11:25.123
When you say `object.something`.

00:11:25.123 --> 00:11:27.115
`getattribute` is always called.

00:11:27.115 --> 00:11:29.545
When&nbsp;you say `object.something = something`.

00:11:29.545 --> 00:11:31.216
`setattr` is always called.

00:11:31.216 --> 00:11:35.664
When you say `del&nbsp;object.something`,
`delattr` is always called.

00:11:35.664 --> 00:11:39.188
And for these three functions,
it's actually&nbsp;the base `object` class

00:11:39.188 --> 00:11:43.380
whose versions of those functions
actually implement the descriptor&nbsp;logic.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:46.920
So if you're defining `__getattribute__`,
`__setattr__`, or `__delattr__`,

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:49.080
then you&nbsp;should call the base `object`'s&nbsp;

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:51.240
version of that function&nbsp;
inside your implementation

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:54.000
if you want it to work with descriptors.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.800
If you don't call the base `object`'s version,

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:59.565
you'll find that your descriptor
methods `get`, `set`, `delete` are not called.

00:11:59.565 --> 00:12:02.595
Unless, of&nbsp;course, you manually
call them in your version of the function.

00:12:02.595 --> 00:12:07.000
That's to say, you can sort of override&nbsp;
the descriptor protocol if you so choose.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:08.820
And then there's `getattr`, which is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:12.000
only called if `getattribute`&nbsp;
raises an `AttributeError`.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.240
So how does `getattribute` decide&nbsp;
what order to do things in?

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:18.780
The object version of `getattribute`
has a series of&nbsp;fallbacks

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:21.934
that it tries to figure out which
one is the best one to call.

00:12:21.934 --> 00:12:25.922
If it finds a descriptor that has&nbsp;
a `get` and a `set` or `delete`,

00:12:25.922 --> 00:12:29.697
then that's called a data descriptor,&nbsp;
and it has the highest priority.

00:12:29.697 --> 00:12:32.831
Next, it checks inside the instance dictionary.

00:12:32.831 --> 00:12:35.880
Then it checks for descriptors&nbsp;
that just have a `get`.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.220
These are called non-data descriptors.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:40.860
Then it checks for variables&nbsp;
found at the class level.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:45.120
Then it'll raise an `AttributeError`, which&nbsp;
triggers the `__getattr__` if it exists.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:46.980
These defaults are definitely not obvious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.500
although they are generally&nbsp;
good for most use cases.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:52.620
It may feel especially&nbsp;
weird&nbsp;that instance variables&nbsp;

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:56.552
are split between these data&nbsp;
versus non-data descriptors.

00:12:56.552 --> 00:13:00.540
The&nbsp;reason that non-data descriptors
get lower priority than instance variables

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:03.840
is because&nbsp;the most
common use case for these is caching.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.700
The descriptor computes some expensive&nbsp;

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:07.860
variable&nbsp;and then saves it&nbsp;
in the instance dictionary.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:12.240
The next time it gets looked up, it's found
in&nbsp;the instance dictionary and isn't recomputed.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.000
This was chosen because of how common caching&nbsp;is.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:18.000
But if you want your descriptor&nbsp;
to always be preferred,

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:21.480
then you can just define a `set` or&nbsp;
`delete` that just does the default thing.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.360
Here's a case where you can see&nbsp;
that priority difference play out.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:27.960
For `x`, I have a data descriptor. It
defines both&nbsp;a `get` and a `set`.

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:29.820
Whereas `y` just has a `get`.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:31.440
Both of our `get`'s return&nbsp;`None`.&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.300
So when we print `X` and&nbsp;
`Y`, we get `None`, `None`.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:37.980
Then for both `x` and `y`, we store&nbsp;
`42` in the instance dictionary.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:41.640
Print them out again, and for `x`,
we get `None`,&nbsp;but for `y`, we get `42`.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:44.400
This is because the data descriptor's `get`, which&nbsp;

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.560
returns `None`, has a&nbsp;higher&nbsp;
priority than the dictionary.

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:49.560
Whereas the non-data&nbsp;
descriptor's `get` is not called&nbsp;

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:52.703
because&nbsp;the dictionary has&nbsp;
higher priority in that case.

00:13:52.703 --> 00:13:54.900
Obviously, the rules are complex, non-intuitive.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:56.700
And I don't expect you to get them in one go.

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.700
I just hope this helps you remember&nbsp;
that there is some subtlety there.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.580
And you might want to look into it if&nbsp;
you're ever defining a descriptor like this.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:05.700
Anyway, thanks for making
it to the&nbsp;end. I hope you enjoyed it.

